.vscode/
*.swp

# Vercel
.vercel

# Claude Code local session data — except shared dev-server launch config
.claude/*
!.claude/launch.json

# Owner's personal business/tax/banking notes. This repo is PUBLIC — these hold
# ABN + CommBank + Stripe-payout strategy and address references, none of which
# belong in a code repo anyone can read (or fork, or find in history later).
# They live on the owner's machine only. See memory: project_business_setup.
BUSINESS_SETUP_GUIDE.md
SETUP_ONE_PAGER.md

# Design/AI tooling installed 2026-08-09 (taste-skill + impeccable).
# Mine, not the product: ~470 files, 10 MB. .claude/* is already ignored above.
.agents/
.codex/
skills-lock.json
.github/agents/
.github/hooks/
.github/skills/

# The impeccable detector's own config + cache. Same reasoning as .agents/ above:
# the tool is mine, not the product. The DECISIONS it records (which findings are
# accepted and why) live in docs/roadmap.md GA-2, which is committed — so the
# reasoning survives even though the machine-readable file does not.
.impeccable/

# The 4K masters behind the three /learn illustrations (2026-08-16). ~47 MB of
# lossless PNG, too heavy for git, and the site ships the 1600x1000 crops in
# web/public/learn/ instead.
#
# ⚠️ IRREPLACEABLE, and that is the whole reason they are kept at all. These are
# generated images and generation is NOT reproducible: the prompts are stored
# beside them and re-running one returns a DIFFERENT picture, not this one. So
# if this folder is lost, the only remaining copies of these exact images are
# the cropped, colour-corrected 1600x1000 versions that are committed — fine for
# the Learn bands, useless for a hero, a share card, a reframe or print.
#
# Ignored, not deleted. Back it up with the owner's own files, not with git.
reference/learn-masters/

# Excel writes a lock file beside any workbook it has open. The article working
# file is reviewed while it is being rebuilt, so this appears and disappears.
~$*

# The UNCOMPRESSED copy of an article's frozen study data (2026-08-30). The
# committed form is the `.json.gz` beside it — same keys, same values, same row
# order, 2.47 MB down to 0.38 MB, which is smaller than the plain file already
# committed for the first article.
#
# Nothing reads this path: `reference/article-drafts/verify/_data.py` is the one
# place that knows how the data is stored, and every audit script asks IT rather
# than opening a file. Unzip a readable copy whenever you want one —
# `python _data.py out.json` — and it lands here, ignored, instead of doubling
# the size of the repo.
reference/drawdown-recovery-*-DATA.json

# A one-time safety net, kept on disk and out of the repo. Taken immediately
# before deleting the 1,754 split_events rows the 2026-08-30 --repull-prices
# catch-up created (see roadmap "Stale prices + dead tickers", finding 4). These
# are DERIVED rows — a log of what the split pipeline did, computed by nothing —
# so a re-pull regenerates them and 776 KB of dead log does not belong in git.
# Safe to delete once the deletion is confirmed good.
reference/split-events-backup-*.json

# Working files for the one-off `--repull-prices` catch-up (2026-08-30): the
# ticker slices handed to each parallel worker and their logs. The flag is part
# of the pipeline and stays; these are just what one run of it was given.
.repull/
